WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.800
We will now talk about
hierarchical clustering,

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:08.110
the last clustering approach we
will discuss in this lecture.

00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:11.722
In practice,
clusters often have subclusters.

00:00:11.722 --> 00:00:14.350
These subclusters often have
other subclusters,

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:18.260
and we want to capture
the stratification of clusters.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:21.860
For example, in this case,
these are two clusters,

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:25.560
but they can be split further
into more and more clusters.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:30.240
To capture this stratification,
biologists construct trees where

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.970
every leaf corresponds to a data point.

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:40.170
Every horizontal line crossing this
tree will result in a set of clusters.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:45.150
For example, this line crossing the tree

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:47.740
at four points will result in four clusters,

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:54.290
while the line crossing the tree at six
points will correspond to six clusters.

00:00:54.290 --> 00:01:01.050
To construct hierarchical clustering
trees, biologists first transform Data, an

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:08.980
n x m expression matrix, into an n x n
similarity matrix or distance matrix.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:13.258
For example, they can construct
the distance matrix by simply computing

00:01:13.258 --> 00:01:16.580
the Euclidean distances between
the points in the data set.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:21.310
As soon as this matrix is constructed,

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:27.330
the hierarchical clustering algorithm
starts from just representing all data

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:32.140
points as leaves in a tree
that is to be constructed.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:36.790
Afterward, the hierarchical
clustering algorithm finds

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:40.360
the smallest distance
in the distance matrix.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:45.610
In this case, it finds that this
distance is realized as the distance

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:50.580
between the third point and
the fifth point in the data set,

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.010
and it connects the corresponding leaves

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:59.694
and forms a new node that is
a parent of these two leaves.

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:06.340
Then, afterwards, it merges these
leaves into a single row of the matrix,

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:10.221
and in the newly constructed matrix
(I will describe later

00:02:10.221 --> 00:02:15.020
how the distances in this newly
constructed matrix are computed),

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.940
it once again finds the shortest distance.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:24.481
In this case, the shortest distance
is realized between

00:02:24.481 --> 00:02:26.010
element two and element four,

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:30.710
and they are also connected to
a parent node by edges.

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:35.263
I will define later how to define
distances from this parent node to

00:02:35.263 --> 00:02:36.170
the leaves.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:40.964
Once again, it collapses
leaves two and four

00:02:40.964 --> 00:02:48.120
into a single node in the tree and
re-computes the matrix again.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:53.040
In the newly constructed matrix,
it once again finds the smallest element

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.850
and continues in this way until
the whole tree is constructed.

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:02.830
In other words, when there is
only a single cluster,

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:06.960
which corresponds to
the root of the tree, left.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:10.657
Here is the pseudocode for
constructing a tree from a distance matrix

00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:13.921
that pretty much follows the
algorithm that I just described,

00:03:13.921 --> 00:03:20.336
specifying some details on how to
compute the distances in the tree.

00:03:20.336 --> 00:03:25.590
The only thing that remains
unaddressed in this pseudocode

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:31.160
is how to compute the distance
between the newly-formed clusters and

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:38.460
the remaining clusters in the current
partition of the tree into clusters.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.700
And there are different
approaches to clustering.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:45.810
For example, one can compute clusters
using average distance between elements

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:51.410
of two clusters or by using minimum
distance between elements of two clusters.

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:53.930
Of course, this may result in
different clustering problems.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:59.641
These are six clusters built
by HierachicalClustering for

00:03:59.641 --> 00:04:05.970
the same yeast dataset that we looked at
when we talked about k-means clustering.

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:10.701
These clusters are often a departing point

00:04:10.701 --> 00:04:15.710
for biologists to decode
difficult regulatory puzzles.

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:18.560
For example,
if you look at the first data set,

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:24.035
you will see that there is a
sudden surge of expression

00:04:24.035 --> 00:04:28.030
at the last checkpoint for
genes in this data set.

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:32.180
When biologists started looking
more carefully at these genes,

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:38.280
they saw that upstream regions of
many genes in this cluster contained

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:45.130
the "carbon source response element" (CSRE)
with the motif shown on this slide.

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:51.550
And by noticing this, they started
to decode the biological puzzle

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:56.791
of yeast being able to switch from

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:04.300
the glucose to ethanol pathway to
the ethanol metabolizing pathway.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:09.485
Indeed, since yeast prefers glucose
over ethanol as an energy source,

00:05:09.485 --> 00:05:11.100
genes responsible for

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:16.628
metabolizing less tasty ethanols
are repressed in the presence of glucose.

00:05:16.628 --> 00:05:20.929
So this carbon source
response element helped

00:05:20.929 --> 00:05:24.820
biologists to decode the puzzle of CSRE.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:29.590
Indeed, it turned out that CSRE
activates the genes when

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:34.730
the yeast runs out of glucose and
starts consuming ethanol.

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:39.310
I think we are done
with the diauxic shift.

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:42.740
Phillip, should we try
this bottle of wine?

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:43.330
&gt;&gt; Sure, I think so.

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:45.660
Thank you.

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:50.750
Our students may think that
we do it after every class.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:51.828
&gt;&gt; Well, we actually do, don't we.

00:05:51.828 --> 00:05:56.110
&gt;&gt; Actually Pavel, come to think of it,
&gt;&gt; you mentioned cancer at the beginning.

00:05:58.070 --> 00:05:59.090
&gt;&gt; What happened with that discussion?

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:00.890
Back to recording.

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:06.620
In this lecture,
we focused on the diauxic shift

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:10.600
rather than medical applications
of expression arrays.

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:18.480
In fact, expression arrays have both hopes
and concerns when it comes to oncology.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.020
For example, gene expression
studies reveals that breast cancer

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:25.980
has more grades than previously thought,

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:30.390
and as a result, there is a possibility
to add a genomic grade

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:34.990
to a traditional histological
classification of breast cancer.

00:06:34.990 --> 00:06:39.470
On the other hand, microarrays allow
doctors to distinguish between

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:43.390
morphologically identical
types of leukemia and

00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:46.390
lead to a better clinical outcome.

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:52.300
However, there are also concerns about
applications of expression arrays

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:54.160
in clinical oncology.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:57.980
First, tumor sample
preparation is critical for

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:02.830
accuracy and reproducibility
of expression arrays,

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:08.110
and standard procedures for tumor sampling
or storage are still in development.

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:15.740
Very important, the bioinformatics model for
classifying tumors should be extremely

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:22.310
accurate if bioinformaticians want them
to be applicable to medical applications.

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:27.600
Indeed, they should have
extremely low false positive and

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:34.479
false negative rates because false
positives lead to unnecessary surgeries,

00:07:34.479 --> 00:07:40.211
while false negatives lead to
missing early stages of cancer.

